{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST be the home of drafting, reviewing, and agreeing requirements, the requirement review criteria, the procedure for choosing references, and the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject says the `habeas-requirements` skill is the home of six topics: drafting, reviewing, agreeing requirements, the requirement review criteria, the procedure for choosing references, and the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests. The parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` is cited as context. It defines a skill as its `SKILL.md` plus its `references/` files, and says each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill.\n\nThe marked test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_is_home_of_drafting_review_references_and_criteria]`. It covers each topic as follows:\n\n- **Drafting, reviewing and agreeing.** It asserts that the `habeas-requirements` workflow steps, read through `workflow_steps`, contain \"Draft. Write drafts\", \"Review. Sort the new and changed requirements\" and \"**Gate:**\". Together these show the skill carries the three steps.\n- **Choosing references.** It asserts that the \"## Choosing references\" section, extracted by `section`, contains \"A *root* is\".\n- **Criteria for code and tests.** It asserts that the \"## What the review checks\" section contains the phrases \"obligations, boundaries, and failure cases no test runs and asserts\" and \"The supervising agent must still check that the code fulfills each applicable requirement\".\n- **Requirement review criteria.** It reads the \"## Criteria\" section of `references/review-prompt.md` and asserts that it contains \"**Compound:**\", \"**Checkable:**\" and \"**One reading:**\". This shows the criteria live in the skill's reference file.\n- **Home, not duplication.** It calls `assert_home(\"habeas-requirements\")`. The helper first asserts that every `home_markers` phrase for this skill appears in the combined `SKILL.md` and references text. Those markers are the drafting, subagent-review, gate, root, \"What the review checks\", review-prompt heading and Compound-criterion phrases. It then asserts that none of those phrases appears in any of the other four skills, using the `SKILLS` list and `bundled_skill`.\n\nThe obligation is placement, and placement is what these assertions enforce. A skill that dropped any of the six topics fails a presence assertion. A topic restated in another skill fails the marker check in `assert_home`. The sibling test `bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics` checks that the skill set is exactly the five directories. The parent requirement's \"exactly one skill\" rule is the parent's, and its test covers it.\n\nI found no clause of the subject that is untested. Each topic is checked through a few distinctive phrases rather than the whole text. That is a reasonable way to test \"home of\" for prose, and I do not count it as a gap. There are no {fixme} acknowledgments to weigh.",
  "references": "f159a23beb16749e13e1d1de7c5be2c78dd6bcfcbb8168129bbd0ec2bacc21bf",
